.TH SETJMP 3
.SH NAME
setjmp, longjmp \- non-local goto
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <setjmp.h>

int
setjmp(env)
jmp_buf env;

longjmp(env, val)
jmp_buf env;
int val;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions deal with errors and interrupts met in a low-level
function of a program.
.PP
.B setjmp
saves its stack environment in
.I env
and returns 0.
.B longjmp
restores the environment saved by the last call of setjmp with
.IR env ,
and the program goes on as if that call of setjmp had just returned,
this time with the value
.I val
(1, if
.I val
is 0).
.PP
The function that called setjmp must not have returned before longjmp
is called.
.SH EXAMPLES
To go back to the main loop of a program when an interrupt arrives:
.PP
.nf
	#include <setjmp.h>
	#include <signal.h>

	jmp_buf top;

	onintr()
	{
		signal(SIGINT, onintr);
		longjmp(top, 1);
	}

	main()
	{
		setjmp(top);
		signal(SIGINT, onintr);
		for (;;)
			command();
	}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
signal(2)
.SH HISTORY
The
.B setjmp
and
.B longjmp
functions first appeared in the Programmer's Workbench, PWB/UNIX.
.SH BUGS
Register variables of the function that called setjmp have, after
longjmp, the values they had when setjmp was called; its other
automatic variables have the values they had when longjmp was called.
